+= recompression
+
+With CONFIG_ZRAM_MULTI_COMP, a zram device can be configured with up
+to three secondary compression algorithms in addition to the primary
+one that is used on the write path.  The secondary algorithms are
+applied to selected objects afterwards (recompression), trading a
+little CPU for a better compression ratio.
+
+The secondary algorithms must be assigned before the device is
+initialised (i.e. before disksize is set):
+
+	# show available algorithms and current assignment
+	cat /sys/block/zram0/recomp_algorithm
+	#1:
+	#2:
+	#3:
+
+	# assign zstd as secondary algorithm #1
+	echo "algo=zstd priority=1" > /sys/block/zram0/recomp_algorithm
+
+	# assign deflate as secondary algorithm #2
+	echo "algo=deflate priority=2" > /sys/block/zram0/recomp_algorithm
+
+Recompression is then requested via the recompress attribute, which
+scans the device and re-compresses matching objects with the
+configured secondary algorithm(s):
+
+	# mark pages as idle, then recompress them
+	echo all > /sys/block/zram0/idle
+	echo "type=idle" > /sys/block/zram0/recompress
+
+	# recompress incompressible (huge) pages, but only those that
+	# are still larger than 3000 bytes
+	echo "type=huge threshold=3000" > /sys/block/zram0/recompress
+
+	# recompress idle pages using only the algorithm assigned to
+	# priority 1, skipping objects already compressed below 2048
+	# bytes
+	echo "type=idle threshold=2048 priority=1" > \
+		/sys/block/zram0/recompress
+
+A secondary algorithm replaces an object only if that results in a
+smaller zsmalloc size class, so recompression never grows the pool.
+Objects that none of the secondary algorithms can shrink are marked
+incompressible and are skipped by subsequent recompression runs.
+
+Note: slots managed by the deduplication feature (CONFIG_ZRAM_DEDUP)
+are never recompressed.
+
 = memory tracking
 
